WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.020
Day 3 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:07.859
The new ways of testing: from asserts to evals.

00:00:08.409 --> 00:00:13.417
How to turn a test with an exact assert on an AI output into a three-layer eval that

00:00:13.417 --> 00:00:14.251
isn't brittle.

00:00:15.551 --> 00:00:19.641
When a feature includes a model, the classic test stops working as is.

00:00:19.941 --> 00:00:24.537
An assert comparing against exact text fails even when the answer is right, because

00:00:24.537 --> 00:00:26.863
the model words it differently every time.

00:00:27.163 --> 00:00:31.670
The usual reaction is to mark the test as flaky, add retries and move on.

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:35.189
That hides the problem: the test stopped measuring.

00:00:36.489 --> 00:00:40.264
The way out isn't abandoning automation but changing the question.

00:00:40.564 --> 00:00:45.551
Instead of "is it equal to this?", we ask "does it meet these criteria, most of the

00:00:45.551 --> 00:00:45.971
time?".

00:00:46.271 --> 00:00:47.423
That is an eval.

00:00:47.723 --> 00:00:51.853
This guide shows how to build one in three layers, from the cheapest and most

00:00:51.853 --> 00:00:55.446
deterministic to the most expensive, and how to choose a threshold.

00:00:56.746 --> 00:00:58.444
To follow this guide you need:

00:00:58.994 --> 00:01:01.098
Node.js 22 or later.

00:01:01.648 --> 00:01:04.611
One of your tests that compares exact text.

00:01:05.161 --> 00:01:07.648
Optional: Ollama with a local model.

00:01:08.948 --> 00:01:10.228
This is the setup.

00:01:10.528 --> 00:01:14.745
Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.

00:01:16.045 --> 00:01:17.081
Step 1.

00:01:17.381 --> 00:01:18.998
Assert versus eval.

00:01:19.548 --> 00:01:24.209
Understand the difference: an assert compares against an exact value; an eval

00:01:24.209 --> 00:01:29.233
measures whether the output meets criteria, several times, and yields a percentage.

00:01:29.783 --> 00:01:34.186
An assert has one input, one expected output and a binary result.

00:01:34.486 --> 00:01:39.335
An eval has a set of inputs, criteria instead of a single output, several runs per

00:01:39.335 --> 00:01:42.824
input and a percentage result compared against a threshold.

00:01:43.124 --> 00:01:48.432
How you read a failure changes too: in an assert, red means bug; in an eval, one bad

00:01:48.432 --> 00:01:51.718
run may be noise and five in a row are a regression.

00:01:52.018 --> 00:01:56.623
That is why you need to record the pass rate over time and not just the latest

00:01:56.623 --> 00:01:57.037
result.

00:01:58.337 --> 00:01:59.535
Step 2.

00:01:59.835 --> 00:02:01.800
Layer one: format.

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:08.202
Layer 1, format: validate structure with deterministic checks (valid JSON, required

00:02:08.202 --> 00:02:09.330
fields, length).

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:13.052
It's the cheapest layer and the one that catches the most.

00:02:13.352 --> 00:02:18.350
Check that the output is valid JSON, that required fields exist with the right type,

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:22.515
that maximum lengths hold and that no text sits outside the structure.

00:02:22.815 --> 00:02:25.987
All of that is solved with a schema and no model call.

00:02:26.287 --> 00:02:31.001
If this layer fails, evaluating the next ones is pointless: an answer your

00:02:31.001 --> 00:02:34.951
application can't parse is already a defect, whatever it says.

00:02:36.251 --> 00:02:37.333
Step 3.

00:02:37.633 --> 00:02:39.342
Layer two: content.

00:02:39.893 --> 00:02:45.536
Layer 2, content: verify key facts with contains or regex, without demanding the

00:02:45.536 --> 00:02:46.594
exact sentence.

00:02:47.144 --> 00:02:49.666
Here you verify facts, not wording.

00:02:49.966 --> 00:02:54.861
If the answer must mention an amount, a date and a status, look for those three with

00:02:54.861 --> 00:02:56.784
contains or a regular expression.

00:02:57.084 --> 00:03:01.644
Checking the opposite also helps: that information which shouldn't be there doesn't

00:03:01.644 --> 00:03:05.051
appear, such as another customer's data or a nonexistent step.

00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:07.815
This layer is still deterministic and cheap.

00:03:08.115 --> 00:03:12.790
The practical rule is that every hard fact gets validated here, never by another

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:13.725
model's opinion.

00:03:15.025 --> 00:03:16.038
Step 4.

00:03:16.338 --> 00:03:18.140
Layer three: judgment.

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:24.237
Layer 3, judgment: use a rubric graded by another model for what rules can't express

00:03:24.237 --> 00:03:25.624
(tone, completeness).

00:03:26.174 --> 00:03:30.587
What remains is what rules can't express: whether the tone fits, whether the

00:03:30.587 --> 00:03:34.884
explanation is complete, whether the answer really addresses the question.

00:03:35.184 --> 00:03:40.028
For that you use a rubric graded by another model, with observable criteria and a

00:03:40.028 --> 00:03:41.224
pass or fail result.

00:03:41.524 --> 00:03:45.660
It's the most expensive and least reliable layer, so keep it small.

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:49.997
Day sixteen shows how to calibrate that judge against your own judgment before

00:03:49.997 --> 00:03:50.618
trusting it.

00:03:51.918 --> 00:03:53.047
Step 5.

00:03:53.347 --> 00:03:54.917
Runs and threshold.

00:03:55.467 --> 00:04:01.160
Run N times and set a threshold: for example, pass if 9 of 10 runs satisfy all three

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:01.634
layers.

00:04:02.184 --> 00:04:06.878
Run each case several times: three for daily work, five or ten when you're about to

00:04:06.878 --> 00:04:07.783
make a decision.

00:04:08.083 --> 00:04:10.013
The threshold depends on risk.

00:04:10.313 --> 00:04:15.292
For the format layer, demand one hundred percent, because broken JSON breaks the

00:04:15.292 --> 00:04:16.039
application.

00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:19.221
For judgment, ninety percent can be reasonable.

00:04:19.521 --> 00:04:23.706
What matters is setting the number before looking at results and writing down why,

00:04:23.706 --> 00:04:26.513
so you don't adjust it afterwards to whatever came out.

00:04:27.813 --> 00:04:29.000
Step 6.

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:31.032
Its place in the pyramid.

00:04:31.582 --> 00:04:36.586
Place it in your pyramid: unit and contract stay deterministic; evals are an extra

00:04:36.586 --> 00:04:39.027
layer for anything that touches a model.

00:04:39.577 --> 00:04:42.122
Evals replace nothing you already have.

00:04:42.422 --> 00:04:47.080
Unit and contract tests stay deterministic and keep running on every commit.

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:52.281
Evals are an extra layer covering only what touches a model, and they usually run at

00:04:52.281 --> 00:04:54.906
a different frequency because they cost more.

00:04:55.206 --> 00:04:59.577
A common mistake is trying to cover logic that doesn't depend on the model with

00:04:59.577 --> 00:05:03.394
evals: that is tested better, faster and cheaper with a classic test.

00:05:04.694 --> 00:05:05.799
An example.

00:05:06.349 --> 00:05:11.527
A team has a test comparing an order summary against fixed text, failing one run in

00:05:11.527 --> 00:05:11.902
three.

00:05:12.202 --> 00:05:13.876
They rewrite it like this.

00:05:14.176 --> 00:05:19.749
Layer one: the output is JSON with summary and status fields, and the summary stays

00:05:19.749 --> 00:05:21.226
within 280 characters.

00:05:21.526 --> 00:05:25.395
Layer two: the summary contains the order number and the total amount.

00:05:25.695 --> 00:05:30.283
Layer three: a rubric checks that it adds no steps the order doesn't have.

00:05:30.583 --> 00:05:32.223
They run it ten times.

00:05:32.523 --> 00:05:37.262
Layer one passes ten out of ten, so does layer two, layer three nine out of ten.

00:05:37.562 --> 00:05:42.430
With a ninety percent threshold it passes, and the failed run is saved for review.

00:05:43.730 --> 00:05:45.392
The most common mistakes.

00:05:45.942 --> 00:05:50.113
Covering an exact assert with retries instead of changing what is measured.

00:05:50.663 --> 00:05:54.996
Using a judge model to validate hard facts such as amounts or dates.

00:05:55.546 --> 00:05:57.964
Setting the threshold after seeing the results.

00:05:58.514 --> 00:06:00.966
Drawing conclusions from a single run.

00:06:02.266 --> 00:06:04.509
To know it is done, check this.

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:08.452
The original test is rewritten into three separate layers.

00:06:09.002 --> 00:06:12.441
Format and content are validated without calling a model.

00:06:12.991 --> 00:06:15.931
There is a written threshold with its justification.

00:06:16.481 --> 00:06:19.526
You saved and reviewed at least one failed run.

00:06:20.826 --> 00:06:22.721
And where AI fell short.

00:06:23.271 --> 00:06:26.106
The judge model approved an answer with a wrong fact.

00:06:26.406 --> 00:06:29.788
Facts get validated with rules, not with another model.

00:06:31.088 --> 00:06:32.541
That was day 3.

00:06:32.841 --> 00:06:37.816
The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently

00:06:37.816 --> 00:06:38.823
asked questions.

00:06:39.373 --> 00:06:44.275
Continue with day 4: Your AI QA lab: full setup in 30 minutes.
